1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,412
Add a Gmail node as the last step.

2
00:00:02,412 --> 00:00:06,839
It offers a Resource field, and one of the options is "Draft." Choose it.

3
00:00:06,839 --> 00:00:10,357
The Operation field then offers "Create." Choose that too.

4
00:00:10,357 --> 00:00:15,087
You have just built the entire safety guarantee of this agent into two clicks.

5
00:00:15,087 --> 00:00:18,120
What do those two words buy, in plain Gmail terms?

6
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:22,790
Every chase the agent writes lands in your drafts folder and nowhere else: no

7
00:00:22,790 --> 00:00:27,702
recipient has seen it, no thread has been started, nothing has left your account.

8
00:00:27,702 --> 00:00:32,311
If you use a different mail provider, the same pairing exists under slightly

9
00:00:32,311 --> 00:00:36,981
different names, and the shopping rule is identical: the resource that stores

10
00:00:36,981 --> 00:00:40,499
without sending is the one this agent is allowed to touch.

11
00:00:40,499 --> 00:00:45,533
Priya's Tuesday now contains a drafts folder with two or three chases waiting, each

12
00:00:45,533 --> 00:00:49,657
one a decision she makes with one read and one click, or one delete.

13
00:00:49,657 --> 00:00:54,327
The agent moved the work to the exact threshold of the irreversible step, and

14
00:00:54,327 --> 00:00:54,812
stopped.

15
00:00:54,812 --> 00:00:57,844
Everything before that line is cheap to get wrong.

16
00:00:57,844 --> 00:01:00,088
Everything after it costs an apology.

17
00:01:00,088 --> 00:01:04,637
The dropdown is where you chose which side of the line this agent lives on.

18
00:01:05,187 --> 00:01:10,209
This is the single most important idea in this entire course, and it shows up here

19
00:01:10,209 --> 00:01:15,232
because a follow up chaser is where the temptation to "just automate the send too"

20
00:01:15,232 --> 00:01:16,028
is strongest.

21
00:01:16,028 --> 00:01:16,640
Resist it.

22
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:21,663
The two clicks you just made are worth more than any sentence you could write in a

23
00:01:21,663 --> 00:01:22,092
prompt.

24
00:01:22,092 --> 00:01:24,786
Put the guarantee in Section 1.3's language.

25
00:01:24,786 --> 00:01:29,931
Everything an agent reads is, functionally, part of its instructions, and you cannot

26
00:01:29,931 --> 00:01:33,545
fully control what arrives in a sheet another person edits.

27
00:01:33,545 --> 00:01:38,384
The missing Send node is different in kind: no sentence any input could contain

28
00:01:38,384 --> 00:01:40,221
wires a node into your canvas.

29
00:01:40,221 --> 00:01:42,243
Briefings bend under weird input.

30
00:01:42,243 --> 00:01:43,223
Wiring does not.

31
00:01:43,223 --> 00:01:48,184
That is why every guardrail in this course that really matters is a thing that is

32
00:01:48,184 --> 00:01:50,450
absent, not a thing that is promised.

33
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,132
Look at that canvas the way Section 1 taught you to: what can this agent reach, and

34
00:01:56,132 --> 00:01:57,059
what can it do.

35
00:01:57,059 --> 00:01:59,161
It can reach one sheet, read only.

36
00:01:59,161 --> 00:02:04,045
It can do exactly one thing with what it finds: write a draft nobody sees until

37
00:02:04,045 --> 00:02:05,653
Priya opens Gmail herself.

38
00:02:05,653 --> 00:02:10,660
Say the audit out loud, both halves, the way you did for the imported template in

39
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:14,494
Section 0, and notice how much faster it goes the fourth time.

40
00:02:14,494 --> 00:02:15,545
Reach: one sheet.

41
00:02:15,545 --> 00:02:16,967
Act: one drafts folder.

42
00:02:16,967 --> 00:02:22,098
That two-line answer is the whole security review, and being able to give it in ten

43
00:02:22,098 --> 00:02:26,859
seconds, for any workflow on your canvas, is the fluency this course has been

44
00:02:26,859 --> 00:02:28,775
building underneath the builds.

45
00:02:29,325 --> 00:02:33,907
You might already be itching to give the agent one more ability: search the mail,

46
00:02:33,907 --> 00:02:36,509
check for a reply, skip the unnecessary chase.

47
00:02:36,509 --> 00:02:38,149
Hold that itch, deliberately.

48
00:02:38,149 --> 00:02:42,787
That exact addition, made carelessly, is Section 8's near miss, and made properly,

49
00:02:42,787 --> 00:02:44,202
it is Section 8's lesson.

50
00:02:44,202 --> 00:02:48,670
The version you have built is the version that earns the right to grow, and the

51
00:02:48,670 --> 00:02:53,308
occasional redundant draft is the modest rent you pay, for now, for an agent whose

52
00:02:53,308 --> 00:02:55,288
reach you can recite in one breath.

53
00:02:55,838 --> 00:02:58,171
Keep both kinds, and know which is which.

54
00:02:58,171 --> 00:03:02,951
Briefing rules are cheap, expressive and right nearly all the time, which makes them

55
00:03:02,951 --> 00:03:04,602
perfect for tone and content.

56
00:03:04,602 --> 00:03:09,211
Wiring rules are blunt, binary and absolute, which makes them the only acceptable

57
00:03:09,211 --> 00:03:12,682
form for anything you cannot afford to have happen even once.

58
00:03:12,682 --> 00:03:17,178
The craft of this whole course fits in one sentence: put each rule in the layer

59
00:03:17,178 --> 00:03:19,113
whose failure you could live with.

60
00:03:19,663 --> 00:03:24,677
Make the verifying concrete, and make it a ritual you also run on other people's

61
00:03:24,677 --> 00:03:24,991
work.

62
00:03:24,991 --> 00:03:30,256
When you import any template, including this course's own C01 to C04, the first move

63
00:03:30,256 --> 00:03:32,136
is not connecting credentials.

64
00:03:32,136 --> 00:03:37,276
It is a slow lap of the canvas reading every node's Resource and Operation, asking

65
00:03:37,276 --> 00:03:41,727
one question per box: what is the worst this node as configured can do?

66
00:03:41,727 --> 00:03:43,106
A minute per workflow.

67
00:03:43,106 --> 00:03:47,807
Trust in this world is not a feeling, it is an inspection you have actually

68
00:03:47,807 --> 00:03:53,072
performed, and people who inspect templates before running them are the people whose

69
00:03:53,072 --> 00:03:56,206
name never ends up on an email they did not write.

70
00:03:56,206 --> 00:03:59,591
One more place the ritual pays: your own future edits.

71
00:03:59,591 --> 00:04:04,543
The riskiest person in this workflow's life is you in three months, adding "one

72
00:04:04,543 --> 00:04:06,611
small thing" on a busy afternoon.

73
00:04:06,611 --> 00:04:11,814
The lap of the canvas is for that person most of all, and the lap after any edit is

74
00:04:11,814 --> 00:04:14,634
the habit that catches what the edit changed.

75
00:04:14,634 --> 00:04:19,900
With the guardrail in place and verified, next lecture connects this agent to a real

76
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:21,091
inbox and ships it.
